@meta
id: 14-01
title: The shape of a substitute
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-basic
par: 30
minutes: 4
vscode: Ctrl-H, type both boxes, click Replace once
open: RUNBOOK.md

@brief
The owner named on the title line of this runbook is out of date. The
worker belongs to `payments-team` now, not `platform-team`. Nothing else
in the file mentions the old name.

Chapter 11 handed you a substitute command written out in full and told
you not to worry about it. This chapter is where you take it apart. A
substitute has four parts: where to look, what to look for, what to put
there, and how strictly. This lesson uses the two in the middle and
leaves the other two at their defaults, which means: this line, first
match only.

The cursor is already on the line.

@keys
| `:s/old/new/` `Enter` | on this line, replace the first `old` with `new` |
| `:[range]s/pattern/replacement/[flags]` | the whole shape — range and flags come later |
| `u` | undo it if the result surprises you |

@hints
You do not need to move. The command names the text; the cursor only
decides which line.
--
The pattern is `platform` and the replacement is `payments`. Three
slashes hold them apart.
--
`:s/platform/payments/` and Enter. The `-team` after it is untouched
because the pattern never mentioned it.

@solution
    :s/platform/payments/<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-six keystrokes for a one-word fix is not a good trade, and it is
not meant to be. `cw` would beat it. What you are buying is the shape,
because every other lesson in this chapter is this shape with one part
filled in differently:

    :[range]s/pattern/replacement/[flags]

Leave the range out and it means the current line. Leave the flags out
and it means the first match on each line. The pattern is a real vim
pattern, the same dialect chapter 13 taught, so anything you can search
for you can substitute. The replacement is mostly literal text, with a
handful of special characters you will meet one at a time.

Read a substitute out loud as a sentence and it stops looking like
line noise: *on this line, where it says platform, say payments*.

@start RUNBOOK.md
# Runbook: order-worker (owner: platform-team)

When the refund queue backs up, restart the worker and watch the depth
fall. It should be empty within two minutes.

1. Check the depth: `./scripts/queue-depth payments.refunds`
2. Stop the worker: `./stop/order-worker`
3. Start it again: `./start/order-worker`
4. Tail the log: `docker logs -f order-worker`

If the depth is still rising after ten minutes, page #payments-oncall.

@expected RUNBOOK.md
# Runbook: order-worker (owner: payments-team)

When the refund queue backs up, restart the worker and watch the depth
fall. It should be empty within two minutes.

1. Check the depth: `./scripts/queue-depth payments.refunds`
2. Stop the worker: `./stop/order-worker`
3. Start it again: `./start/order-worker`
4. Tail the log: `docker logs -f order-worker`

If the depth is still rising after ten minutes, page #payments-oncall.
